The origins of this book are snSiciently unusual to be of interest, and
they bear a direct relation to its contents and what it sets out to do. So
let

me teU you just how it came to be written.
One day in the spring of 1959 the British Broadcasting Corporation

invited

me to interview for British television Dr. Carl Gustav Jimg.

The interview was to be done “in depth.” I knew little enough at that
time about Jung and his work, and I at once went to make his
acquaintance at his beautiful lakeside home near Zurich. That was the
beginning of a friendship that meant a great deal to me and, I hope,

gave some pleasure to Jung in the last years of his life. The television
intervaew has no further place in this story, except that it was accounted
successful and that this book is by an odd combination of circumstances

an end-product of that success.
